number|title|condition|evidence|remediation|limitation|attack|nist|nist_rationale|sources|severity
001|A lower-trust administrator controls a trusted federation host|An unapproved principal administers an AD FS or other supported on-premises issuer host AND that issuer is actively trusted for tenant authentication.|Domain federation configuration; issuer binding; host inventory; effective administration rights; approved boundary.|Protect federation hosts as identity control-plane assets and remove lower-trust administration.|An installed AD FS role that is not trusted by the assessed tenant is not this correlation.|T1556.007;T1606.002|AC-6;IA-2;SC-7|Protect the issuer that participates in cloud authentication.|MS-FED-MFA|High
002|Federation signing-key access crosses an administrative boundary|An unapproved principal can use or obtain the active federation token-signing private key through effective key or service permissions AND the tenant trusts that issuer.|Signing certificate thumbprint; key provider and usage restrictions; key ACL metadata; service access; trusted issuer.|Restrict signing-key use and protect the signing service; plan rotation if unauthorized exposure is confirmed.|A public certificate is not a private key. Non-exportable or HSM-backed keys require evaluation of signing-use permission, not export flags alone.|T1606.002|AC-6;IA-5;SC-12|Protect cryptographic signing authority used to assert cloud identity.||High
003|Federation key-protection material has an indirect control path|An unapproved principal has effective access to AD FS DKM protection material AND a separately evidenced path reaches the encrypted signing material or its service.|DKM object ACL; service/account relationship; encrypted-material location metadata; effective host or file access; issuer linkage.|Restrict both protection-material access and the associated service or storage path.|DKM readability alone does not prove the signing key can be recovered. Do not read or decrypt any key material.|T1606.002|AC-3;IA-5;SC-12|Protect the combined authorization boundary around signing-key protection material.||High
004|A weakly controlled issuer can assert cloud MFA satisfaction|FEDERATION.001 or .002 is established AND the effective federation and Conditional Access configuration accepts that issuer's MFA claim for a sensitive sign-in scenario.|Issuer control evidence; federatedIdpMfaBehavior; relevant claims rules; effective CA scope and authentication requirements.|Protect issuer administration and verify which MFA claims the cloud should accept.|Federation does not inherently bypass MFA. Cloud-enforced authentication strength or rejected issuer claims may break this specific path.|T1556.006;T1606.002|IA-2;IA-5;AC-3|Verify the independence and trustworthiness of asserted authentication factors.|MS-FED-MFA;MS-CA|High
005|An unapproved administrator controls an active PTA agent|A principal outside the protected boundary administers a host registered as an active pass-through authentication agent for the tenant.|Agent registration and status; host identity; effective administrators; service identity; tenant authentication mode.|Protect PTA hosts and restrict agent registration and administration.|Do not infer PTA from software presence alone. Never patch the agent or test credential interception.|T1556.007|AC-6;IA-2;SI-7|Protect the integrity of the process that validates cloud sign-in against AD.||High
006|Seamless SSO account control is delegated too broadly|An unapproved principal can reset or take effective control of the AZUREADSSOACC account AND Seamless SSO is enabled for that forest and tenant.|Exact account SID; effective ACL; SSO forest configuration; account status; approved operator list.|Restrict SSO account control and follow supported key-rotation procedures when remediation is approved.|The account name alone is not proof of active SSO. An old account without an active trust remains a separate posture issue.|T1556.007;T1558|AC-3;AC-6;IA-5|Protect the account and key lifecycle behind the hybrid SSO trust.||High
007|A synced operator can alter the tenant's federation trust|An AD-controllable Entra principal has effective authorization to modify the assessed domain federation configuration.|Source control path; verified cloud identity; effective cloud role or API permission; domain scope; current trust.|Separate federation administration from AD-controlled identities and limit trust-management grants.|Role names alone are insufficient; custom roles, app permissions, and domain scope must be resolved.|T1484.002;T1098|AC-3;AC-6;CM-5|Restrict who can change the trust that determines authentication authority.|MS-APP-PERMS|High
008|Federation service-account control reaches the signing service|An unapproved principal can control the federation service account AND effective service or key-use authorization makes that account part of the active token-signing path.|Service-to-account binding; effective account control; signing-use permissions; active issuer configuration.|Protect the service identity and narrow signing-service permissions.|Do not assume service-account reset reveals existing secrets or permits immediate signing; independent key-use restrictions must be evaluated.|T1098;T1606.002|AC-6;IA-5;SC-12|Protect service authenticators and the separate authorization to use signing keys.||High
